---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Setting Up Your Research - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="competitive-research" subChapterId="setting-up-your-research">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				The biggest mistake people make with AI-assisted competitive research is starting with brief or vague requirements like "tell me about our competitors."
			</p>

			<h3>Start With Specific Questions</h3>

			<p>
				Before you open an AI chat, write down 3 to 5 specific strategic questions you actually need answered. These should be questions that will drive a decision, not broad curiosity. The more focused your questions, the more useful the AI's output will be.
			</p>

			<p>
				Formatting these questions into a markdown file can help you organize your questions but also set your intended goals with the research. Are we trying to modernize their SDLC? Are we working to reduce their costs? Are we trying to improve their sales process?
			</p>

			<p>Compare these two approaches:</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="warning" title="Vague (unhelpful)">
					<p class="italic">
						"Tell me about competitors in the project management space."
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="success" title="Focused (actionable)">
					<p class="italic">
						"We're building a project management tool for agencies with 10-50 people. I need to understand: 1) Which competitors specifically target agencies vs. general teams? 2) What do their pricing tiers look like for that team size? 3) What features do they highlight for client-facing work?"
					</p>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<p>
				The second prompt gives the AI your market segment, team size, and three concrete angles to research.
			</p>

			<h3>Give the AI Context About You</h3>

			<p>
				The AI doesn't know anything about your company unless you tell it. Before asking about competitors, give it a brief picture of who you are and what you're comparing against. This doesn't need to be long. A few sentences is usually enough.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="info" title="Example prompt">
				<p>
					"We're a B2B SaaS company that provides time tracking and resource planning for digital agencies. Our main differentiator is that we integrate directly with Jira and Confluence. Our typical customer has 15-40 employees. I want to understand how Harvest, Toggl, and Float position themselves against companies like us."
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<p>
				That context changes everything about the quality of the response. Without it, the AI gives you a Wikipedia-style overview. With it, the AI can focus on the angles that actually matter to your business.
			</p>

			<h3>Avoid Leading Questions</h3>

			<p>
				Context about who you are is useful. Context about what you expect the answer to be is not. AI models tend to agree with the person asking, and they pick up on small cues. If your prompt hints at a conclusion, the AI will usually find evidence for it and skip the evidence against it. The result reads like research but is closer to an echo of your own opinion.
			</p>

			<p>
				The framing doesn't have to be obvious to have an effect. Words like "weaker," "outdated," or "struggling" in the question, or phrasing like "Why is X losing customers?", tell the AI what answer you want.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="warning" title="Leading">
					<p class="italic">
						"Float's pricing seems expensive for small agencies. How does it compare to ours?"
					</p>
					<p class="italic">
						"Why are agencies switching from Harvest to newer tools?"
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="success" title="Neutral">
					<p class="italic">
						"Compare Float's pricing to ours for an agency of 15-40 people. Include where each one is cheaper."
					</p>
					<p class="italic">
						"Are agencies switching away from Harvest? Look for evidence in both directions and tell me if it's inconclusive."
					</p>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<p>
				The second leading example assumes the switching is happening. The neutral version makes the AI establish that first. If you do have a hypothesis, state it as one and ask the AI to argue against it: "My assumption is X. What evidence would contradict it?"
			</p>

			<p>
				The same applies to follow-ups. Replying "Are you sure? I thought Toggl was cheaper" will often get the AI to change its answer whether or not it was wrong. Ask it to recheck the source instead.
			</p>

			<h3>Use Web-Enabled AI Tools</h3>

			<p>
				For competitive research, you need an AI tool that can search the web. Without web access, the AI is limited to whatever was in its training data, which has a cutoff date and will be far more likely to be hallucinating information.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Modern tools usually decide on their own whether to search, but they don't always search when you would want them to. For research, being explicit removes the guesswork: a prompt like "Search the web for Competitor X's current pricing page" reliably gets fresher data than just asking "What does Competitor X charge?" When a question is broad enough to need many sources, reach for a{" "}
					<a href="/competitive-research/deep-research" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">Deep Research</a> mode instead.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Prepare Your Reference Material</h3>

			<p>
				If you have specific pages, documents, or links you want the AI to analyze, gather them before you start. Feeding the AI a competitor's actual pricing page or feature list produces far better analysis than asking it to recall that information from memory.
			</p>

			<p>Good reference material to have ready:</p>

			<ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
				<li>Your own product's feature list or positioning page</li>
				<li>Competitor URLs you want analyzed (pricing pages, feature pages, landing pages)</li>
				<li>Any existing internal research your team has</li>
				<li>The specific deliverable you need to produce (presentation, document, Confluence page)</li>
			</ul>

			<p>
				Think of it like briefing a new analyst on their first day. The more context you provide up front, the less time you spend correcting and redirecting later.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="info" title="Remember">
				<p>
					AI is excellent at organizing, comparing, and summarizing information you give it. It can search the web, but won't have any access to internal documents or information unless you provide it.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
  